find the modular inverse of \\(17\\) modulo \\(83\\), we need to find an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}\\). This can be done using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which finds the greatest common divisor (gcd) of two numbers and expresses the gcd as a linear combination of those numbers.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the gcd of \\(17\\) and \\(83\\):\n\n\\[\n83 = 4 \\cdot 17 + 15\n\\]\n\\[\n17 = 1 \\cdot 15 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n15 = 7 \\cdot 2 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n2 = 2 \\cdot 1 + 0\n\\]\n\nThe gcd is the last non-zero remainder, which is \\(1\\). Since the gcd is \\(1\\), \\(17\\) and \\(83\\) are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express \\(1\\) as a combination of \\(17\\) and \\(83\\):\n\nStarting from the second last equation:\n\\[\n1 = 15 - 7 \\cdot 2\n\\]\nSubstitute \\(2\\) from the previous equation \\(2 = 17 - 1 \\cdot 15\\):\n\\[\n1 = 15 - 7 \\cdot (17 - 1 \\cdot 15) = 15 - 7 \\cdot 17 + 7 \\cdot 15 = 8 \\cdot 15 - 7 \\cdot 17\n\\]\nNow substitute \\(15\\) from the first equation \\(15 = 83 - 4 \\cdot 17\\):\n\\[\n1 = 8 \\cdot (83 - 4 \\cdot 17) - 7 \\cdot 17 = 8 \\cdot 83 - 32 \\cdot 17 - 7 \\cdot 17 = 8 \\cdot 83 - 39 \\cdot 17\n\\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[\n1 = 8 \\cdot 83 - 39 \\cdot 17\n\\]\nThus, \\(-39 \\cdot 17 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}\\). Since we want the inverse to be a positive residue, we convert \\(-39\\) to a positive residue by adding \\(83\\):\n\\[\n-39 + 83 = 44\n\\]\nTherefore, the inverse of \\(17\\) modulo \\(83\\) is \\(44\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{44}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{44}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:3"}
